¼ƒ«&†½šOfzÕ5/Š¤Nå„ùêÄK>€cE'f{"rendered":{"html":"<p>A GCSL workspace is a directory tree rooted at <code>workspace.gcsl</code>. Files in\none directory share public declarations. Child directories inherit the\npublic declarations above them, so common types and helpers can live at\nthe root without repeated imports.</p>\n<p>The marker is an ordinary GCSL source file. It can be empty or declare\nshared values and functions. The disk loader finds the nearest enclosing\nmarker when you run a file, reads the workspace, and checks every source\nbefore executing the selected file and its dependencies.</p>\n<h2 id=\"workspaces--public-and-private-names\">Public and private names <a class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#workspaces--public-and-private-names\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>Fields, <code>def</code> functions, and declared types contribute to a directory's\npublic scope. <code>let</code>, <code>let mut</code>, and <code>fn</code> bindings remain private to their\nfile. Dotted public paths merge when they define distinct leaves; a\nconflicting leaf is an error unless an earlier declaration explicitly\npermits shadowing.</p>\n<p>Sibling and child directories are not automatically in scope. Imports\nname paths from the workspace root, with <code>/</code> between directories, as the\nfile system does. <code>import lib/helpers</code> names <code>lib/helpers.gcsl</code> or a\ndirectory of that path. Use <code>as</code> for a local alias, or import a named\nmember with <code>from</code>.</p>\n<p>A directory or file name in a path is written as it is, dots and all, so\n<code>import pkgs/net.busybox.busybox as busybox</code> names the directory\n<code>pkgs/net.busybox.busybox</code>. An import without <code>as</code> is named after the\npath's last part, so that part must be a plain name; give a name such as\n<code>net.busybox.busybox</code> one with <code>as</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"workspaces--boundaries-and-evaluation-order\">Boundaries and evaluation order <a class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#workspaces--boundaries-and-evaluation-order\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>Hidden names and nested directories containing their own <code>workspace.gcsl</code>\nare excluded from an enclosing workspace's source discovery. A nested\nworkspace is independent, not an importable child module of the outer\nworkspace. Symbolic-link directory traversal is not a mechanism for\nextending the source tree.</p>\n<p>Dependencies determine file order; path order breaks ties. A file runs\nas a whole before a file that depends on it. Cyclic file dependencies are\nerrors, including cycles introduced through function bodies or imports.</p>\n<p>A standalone file outside any workspace can import standard modules but\ncannot import arbitrary neighboring source files. Create a workspace\nmarker when several files need to cooperate.</p>\n<h2 id=\"workspaces--deliberate-overrides\">Deliberate overrides <a class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#workspaces--deliberate-overrides\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p><code>#[shadowable]</code> marks a public declaration or import as replaceable by a\nlater declaration in the workspace's defined order. The implicit prelude\nimport is shadowable, so your own declarations can use its names.</p>\n<p>Use this attribute for intentional defaults. It is not an escape from\nall conflicts: an ordinary public leaf still cannot be defined twice.\nWhole-value overrides replace the old value, while distinct dotted\nleaves can merge. See <code>gcsl(5)</code> for ordering and import syntax.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"workspaces--public-and-private-names","title":"Public and private names","number":null},{"level":2,"id":"workspaces--boundaries-and-evaluation-order","title":"Boundaries and evaluation order","number":null},{"level":2,"id":"workspaces--deliberate-overrides","title":"Deliberate overrides","number":null}],"has_mermaid":false,"dangling":[],"warnings":[],"broken":[]},"images":[],"links":{"pages":[],"anchors":[]},"refs":null}